      <div class="callout warning"><strong>Important limit:</strong> packaging details change between production runs, markets, and years, and counterfeiters copy whatever is current. This page is a risk framework, not an authentication certificate. If a bottle matters financially, have it verified by the producer or a licensed professional.</div>

      <h2 id="four">The four fraud types</h2>
      <p>Most advice about fake Moutai treats it as one problem. It is not, and the four types require different defences.</p>

      <h3>1. Counterfeit bottles</h3>
      <p>A complete fabrication: fake bottle, fake label, fake box, cheap spirit inside — or worse, industrial alcohol. These are the ones the news reports, and they are the most dangerous. They are also the easiest to avoid, because they almost always enter through channels with no traceable import route and prices far below market.</p>

      <h3>2. Refilled genuine bottles</h3>
      <p>The most insidious type, and increasingly common. A genuine empty bottle — often collected from restaurants and banquets — is refilled with a cheaper baijiu and resealed. The bottle, label, and box are all real. Only the liquid is not.</p>
      <p>This is why "the packaging looked perfect" proves nothing. Defences that work here are about provenance and seals, not printing quality: an unbroken cap ring, a seal that has not been re-glued, and a seller who can account for the bottle's path.</p>

      <h3>3. Lookalike product lines</h3>
      <p>Not counterfeit, but not what the buyer thinks. A bottle carries a prestigious name association while being a different product line — different liquid, different price tier, different status. This is legal, common, and responsible for a large share of "I paid a lot for Moutai" disappointment.</p>
      <p>The defence is simple: identify the <em>exact product</em>, not the family name. "Moutai" as a brand covers a range from everyday series products to the flagship Flying Fairy, and the gap in both liquid and price is enormous.</p>

      <h3>4. Invented vintage and "aged" claims</h3>
      <p>Baijiu labelling does not work like whisky labelling. A number on a Moutai box often refers to a release year, a commemorative edition, or the age of one component in a blend — not to a single-vintage bottling aged that many years in cask. Sellers frequently exploit this ambiguity, and overseas buyers who assume Scotch conventions are the easiest target.</p>
      <p>Defence: require a specific, checkable claim. "Aged 30 years" with no documentation is marketing. A named edition with a verifiable release year is a fact.</p>

