      <h2 id="price">Price as a signal</h2>
      <p>Moutai Flying Fairy (飞天茅台) carries an official retail guide price of ¥1,499 for 500 ml, and has traded well above that in the secondary market for years because supply is limited relative to gifting and investment demand. Outside China, expect $250–600 depending on market and vintage.</p>
      <p>That market structure produces a clean signal: <strong>a genuine premium Moutai has no reason to be sold cheaply by an unknown seller.</strong> A bottle at 40% below prevailing market is not a bargain — it is either a counterfeit, a refill, or a bottle with a story that will not survive inspection.</p>
      <p>The inverse also applies. A price far <em>above</em> market is not proof of authenticity either; it is simply a markup, and it is common in tourist areas and hotel gift shops.</p>

      <h2 id="seller">Seller and channel</h2>
      <p>The most effective single question is not about the bottle. It is: <em>who is legally responsible for this sale, and can I find them?</em></p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Best:</strong> licensed spirits retailer, authorised distributor, established importer with a physical address, or the producer's own channel.</li>
        <li><strong>Acceptable with care:</strong> large online retailer with a returns policy, airport duty-free.</li>
        <li><strong>High risk:</strong> private resellers, social-media sellers, marketplace listings with stock photography, "a friend of a friend" with a case in the trunk.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A legitimate seller can answer three questions without hesitation: where the bottle came from, what the exact product line is, and what happens if it turns out to be wrong. If any of those produces a story instead of an answer, walk away.</p>

      <h2 id="physical">Physical checks</h2>
      <p>These are supporting evidence, not proof. Work through them in order, and treat any single failure as a reason to stop.</p>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>What to look for</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Official verification</td><td>QR code and NFC chip on the cap, checked through the producer's own anti-counterfeit system</td><td>The only check the producer controls directly. Note that a genuine code on a refilled bottle will still verify — it proves the bottle, not the liquid</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cap and seal</td><td>Cap ring intact and unbroken; no evidence of re-gluing, heat, or cutting; seal sits flush</td><td>Refills require the seal to be defeated and restored</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Fill level</td><td>Consistent with the stated volume; not visibly low</td><td>A low fill on a "new" bottle suggests decanting or evaporation from a compromised seal</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Print quality</td><td>Sharp text, correct characters, even colour registration, no fuzzy edges</td><td>Catches crude counterfeits; tells you nothing about refills</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Label alignment</td><td>Straight, evenly applied, correct adhesive behaviour</td><td>Hand-applied replacement labels usually sit slightly off</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Standard code</td><td>GB/T 18356 on a genuine Moutai product</td><td>Confirms you are looking at a geographical-indication product rather than a different line</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Importer label</td><td>Present and legible outside China, with a lookup-able company</td><td>No label means no traceable import route and no recourse</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>One caution about verification codes: counterfeiters have produced fake verification pages, and genuine codes can be harvested from real bottles. Always reach the verification system through the producer's official app or website rather than a link or QR printed by the seller.</p>

      <h2 id="lines">Product lines and lookalikes</h2>
      <p>Maotai's corporate family includes a wide range of products at very different price points, from everyday series bottles to the flagship Flying Fairy. None of the lower-tier products are fake. They are simply not what a buyer imagining "Moutai" usually has in mind.</p>
      <p>The practical consequence for buyers: <strong>learn the product name, not the brand name.</strong> A box that says Moutai and costs $40 is a different product from a box that says Moutai and costs $400, and the difference is not packaging.</p>
      <p>This matters most in gifting. A recipient who knows baijiu will read the exact product instantly, and an ambiguous gift can read as either ignorance or an attempt at value substitution. Buy the product you can name precisely.</p>

      <h2 id="vintage">Vintage and "aged" claims</h2>
      <p>Chinese baijiu does improve with age in the bottle to a degree — unlike wine, it does not turn, and esterification continues slowly — but the market's vintage language is loose.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>A year on the box</strong> usually marks the release or a commemorative occasion, not the distillation date.</li>
        <li><strong>"Aged 15 years"</strong> typically means the blend includes spirit of that age, not that the whole bottle is 15 years old.</li>
        <li><strong>"Old bottle"</strong> claims in resale are almost unverifiable without documented provenance.</li>
        <li><strong>Auction and collector tiers</strong> exist and are legitimate, but they are a specialist market with specialist authentication. Do not enter it casually.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="checklist">The checklist</h2>
      <p>Before you pay for any premium baijiu:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Can I name the exact product line — not the brand family?</li>
        <li>Is the seller licensed and identifiable, with an address I can look up?</li>
        <li>Does the price sit inside the plausible market band?</li>
        <li>Is the cap ring and seal intact, with no sign of rework?</li>
        <li>Does the official verification check pass through the producer's own system?</li>
        <li>If overseas, is there an importer label naming a real company?</li>
        <li>Can the seller explain the supply route in one sentence?</li>
        <li>Would I be comfortable if the recipient had this bottle inspected?</li>
      </ol>
      <p>If any answer is no, do not buy. This is not a category where a good deal compensates for a bad channel.</p>

      <div class="callout"><strong>A cheaper way to learn the style:</strong> if what you actually want is to know whether you like sauce aroma, buy <a href="/db/variety/xijiu/">Xijiu</a> or <a href="/db/variety/langjiu/">Langjiu</a> at $30–60. Both are serious sauce-aroma producers, both are far harder to counterfeit profitably, and both will answer the flavour question that Moutai's price tag is standing in for.</div>

      <section class="faq" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently asked</h2>
        <h3>Can photos prove a bottle is real?</h3>
        <p>No. Photos can expose crude counterfeits — wrong characters, poor printing, obvious seal damage — but they cannot distinguish a refilled genuine bottle from a genuine one, and counterfeiters photograph genuine bottles for their listings. Provenance is the only thing that scales.</p>
        <h3>Is every cheap Moutai fake?</h3>
        <p>No. Lower-tier products in the Moutai corporate family legitimately sell for far less than Flying Fairy, and older or damaged bottles sometimes sell at a discount. But a <em>flagship</em> bottle priced far below market from an unknown seller should be assumed problematic until proven otherwise.</p>
        <h3>Does the anti-counterfeit QR code guarantee authenticity?</h3>
        <p>It verifies the code, not the contents. A genuine bottle can be refilled and its genuine code will still check out. Use the code as one input alongside channel, seal integrity, and price.</p>
        <h3>Are Moutai series products the same as Flying Fairy?</h3>
        <p>No. They are different products at different price points with different liquid. They are legitimate baijiu, but a buyer expecting flagship status and paying series-product prices — or the reverse — is the most common misunderstanding in the category.</p>
        <h3>What should I do if I think I bought a fake?</h3>
        <p>Do not drink it — counterfeit spirits have caused serious poisonings. Keep the bottle and packaging intact, keep your receipt, and report it to the retailer and to your local consumer protection or food safety authority. In China, report to the market supervision bureau (12315).</p>
        <h3>Is it safer to buy Moutai in China?</h3>
        <p>Not automatically. Counterfeits are most prevalent where demand is highest, which is China itself. What helps is buying from an authorised channel — the producer's own stores, major licensed retailers, or official e-commerce flagships — anywhere in the world.</p>
